  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \n\nOngoing light precipitation will taper off as a system pulls \naway from the area this morning. A shortwave may bring \nadditional cloud cover to the area later this morning into the \nearly afternoon; however, little to no precipitation is expected\ncourtesy of staunch dry low-level air that infiltrates the area\ntoday. Once the shortwave departs to the east, a combination of\nweak ridging and dry low-level air will continue to facilitate \ndrier weather through Sunday morning.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \n\nPrecipitation chances are expected to increase as a low pressure \nsystem approaches from the west Sunday afternoon, then ushers a cold \nfront across the forecast area Sunday night. As far as \nprecipitation type goes, temperatures will initially be mild \nenough for precipitation to fall as rain. A transition to snow \nis, however, expected to occur in the mountains as colder air \nsweeps in behind the axis of an upper trough early Monday \nmorning. \n\nBlustery winds should also accompany this system, with \npotential for 20-35 MPH gusts at times, and perhaps even \nadvisory level gusts along the higher elevations of the \nnortheast mountains late Sunday into Monday.\n\nAny lingering upslope showers will come to an end as dry air \nbuilds and prompts a period of drier weather from Tuesday \nthrough Thursday.",
